Notebook[{
Cell["ArrayMultiply", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"477f913b-899a-f04c-ae6b-cd8f18f71c99"],

Cell[TextData[{
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox[
   RowBox[{"ArrayMultiply", "[", 
    RowBox[{
     RowBox[{"{", 
      RowBox[{"arr1", ",", " ", "arr2", ",", " ", "..."}], "}"}], ",", " ", 
     "spec"}], "]"}], "InlineCode"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "15d6f553-5aa9-1a44-b571-350c4b8a4b1b"],
 " ",
 "contracts the given arrays according to the index specification spec, in \
the manner of Einstein summation."
}], "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"3c8ca791-ff2e-3146-b8e4-67c4987df169"],

Cell["Details and Options", "NotesSection",ExpressionUUID->"d6b93f01-674f-0e48-92b7-632a12544eb0"],

Cell["\<\
The specification may be written as a string, \"ij,jk->ik\", in which each \
character is an index label, or as a rule between lists of labels, {{1, 2}, \
{2, 3}} -> {1, 3}.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"b128d687-5baf-6940-84c4-3c4648416b94"],

Cell["\<\
Indices named in the output part survive; every other index is summed over. \
With the output part omitted, the indices that occur exactly once survive, in \
sorted order, and the rest are summed.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"5f558830-7e23-cb4c-883e-71f02d30aadb"],

Cell["\<\
An index may occur in any number of the arrays, and may occur in the output \
as well as in the inputs. That covers diagonals and three-way contractions, \
which a pairwise contraction could not express.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"12dfa676-352a-4442-989f-1421b0ae6031"],

Cell["\<\
The arrays may be given as array objects or as plain nested lists. A symbolic \
array comes through as its list of lists of terms, and the result is the list \
of lists of expressions the contraction builds; a numerical one gives back a \
NumericalArray.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"8d071c5b-7a11-664a-88d3-b69ad2ed81ac"],

Cell["\<\
Contracting every index away leaves a single entry. For numerical arrays that \
comes back as the 0-array, a NumericalArray with no indices, and Normal gives \
the scalar itself; for symbolic ones it is the expression.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c10220b6-3ba7-1e41-9a96-b5fc7222f809"],

Cell["\<\
An array of order zero is named by an empty group of labels, so that \
\",ij->ij\" multiplies a matrix by a scalar. The output part may equally be \
written empty, \"ij,ji->\", to ask for every index to be contracted away.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"d6bc48eb-e6a6-a047-b872-2494e430bac1"],

Cell["\<\
Every array in one call must be of the same kind. Numerical and symbolic \
arrays cannot be contracted together, since the result would be neither and \
its entries would have no consistent reading; attempting it gives \
ArrayMultiply::mixed.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"c4139b5a-c254-9043-b0cc-b7030033dd2d"],

Cell["\<\
The number of labels given for an array must equal its number of indices, and \
every occurrence of a label must have the same length, or the corresponding \
message is produced and $Failed returned.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"ccd2dfb2-e4ea-f04d-bc6f-cc2109dac8ce"],

Cell["Examples", "PrimaryExamplesSection",ExpressionUUID->"132679cb-aba1-2c48-9f7c-60f057cb68d2"],

Cell["The ordinary matrix product:", "ExampleText",ExpressionUUID->"40fa1839-1cd0-0d46-af13-54681ef29559"],

Cell["\<\
ArrayMultiply[{{{1, 2}, {3, 4}}, {{0, 1}, {1, 0}}}, \"ij,jk->ik\"]\
\>", "Input",ExpressionUUID->"3ab61527-b1dc-c94f-8701-99daa64c7a9a"],

